// Compiled by bin/build-help.php from help/*.md (the add-on's topics only). Do not edit.
export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"addon":"circuit","name":"Circuit simulation","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"circuit-start","scope":"addon:circuit","title":"Circuit simulation: getting started","summary":"Draw a circuit as a schematic in CadCraft and simulate it with the Circuit simulation add-on: an operating point, a transient and an AC sweep, read on a meter, a scope and a plotter.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["circuit","schematic","simulation","spice","resistor","capacitor","op-amp","transistor","scope","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"your-first-circuit","text":"Your first circuit","level":2},{"id":"drawing-your-own","text":"Drawing your own","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECircuit simulation is an add-on for CadCraft. It is free and off until you switch it on in Settings, in the add-ons list. It has a schematic editor with a parts palette and a solver of our own that works out the voltages and currents of what you drew.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen it from the right panel (the section named Circuit simulation, with a Larger window button), or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECIRCUIT\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-first-circuit\"\u003EYour first circuit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cstrong\u003EExample\u003C/strong\u003E. It draws a pulse source, a 1 kΩ resistor and a 1 µF capacitor, with a scope and a plotter on the output net.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cstrong\u003EOperating point\u003C/strong\u003E to see the voltage of every net and the current in every part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cstrong\u003ETransient\u003C/strong\u003E to see the capacitor charge on the scope. Press \u003Cstrong\u003EAC sweep\u003C/strong\u003E to see the low-pass response on the plotter: the level falls 3 dB at 159 Hz.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a part, then change its value under the drawing. Run again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawing-your-own\"\u003EDrawing your own\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlace a part.\u003C/strong\u003E Press a part in the palette, then click the drawing. Esc stops placing. Parts snap to a grid.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWire.\u003C/strong\u003E Click a pin, then another pin. Click empty space for a junction, and the wire carries on from it. Click a wire to join to it: a junction is put on the wire. Double-click or Esc ends the wire.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMove, turn, delete.\u003C/strong\u003E Drag a part. R turns it. Delete removes the part or the wire you selected. Undo and Redo are in the row of buttons (also Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EName a net.\u003C/strong\u003E Press \u003Cstrong\u003ENet name\u003C/strong\u003E, then click a pin. Two pins with the same name are on one net. A net named 0 or gnd is ground.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGround.\u003C/strong\u003E Every circuit needs one. Place a Ground part on the net that is your zero volts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESwitch.\u003C/strong\u003E Double-click a switch to open or close it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour drawing is kept in this browser. \u003Cstrong\u003ESave\u003C/strong\u003E writes a file, \u003Cstrong\u003EOpen\u003C/strong\u003E reads one, \u003Cstrong\u003EExport netlist\u003C/strong\u003E writes a text deck of the elements.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is Circuit simulation is an add-on for CadCraft. It is free and off until you switch it on in Settings, in the add-ons list. It has a schematic editor with a parts palette and a solver of our own that works out the voltages and currents of what you drew. Open it from the right panel (the section named Circuit simulation, with a Larger window button), or type CIRCUIT. Your first circuit Press Example. It draws a pulse source, a 1 kΩ resistor and a 1 µF capacitor, with a scope and a plotter on the output net. Press Operating point to see the voltage of every net and the current in every part. Press Transient to see the capacitor charge on the scope. Press AC sweep to see the low-pass response on the plotter: the level falls 3 dB at 159 Hz. Click a part, then change its value under the drawing. Run again. Drawing your own Place a part. Press a part in the palette, then click the drawing. Esc stops placing. Parts snap to a grid. Wire. Click a pin, then another pin. Click empty space for a junction, and the wire carries on from it. Click a wire to join to it: a junction is put on the wire. Double-click or Esc ends the wire. Move, turn, delete. Drag a part. R turns it. Delete removes the part or the wire you selected. Undo and Redo are in the row of buttons (also Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y). Name a net. Press Net name, then click a pin. Two pins with the same name are on one net. A net named 0 or gnd is ground. Ground. Every circuit needs one. Place a Ground part on the net that is your zero volts. Switch. Double-click a switch to open or close it. Your drawing is kept in this browser. Save writes a file, Open reads one, Export netlist writes a text deck of the elements."},{"slug":"circuit-analyses","scope":"addon:circuit","title":"Circuit simulation: analyses and instruments","summary":"How to run the operating point, the transient and the AC sweep, and how to read them on the meter, the scope and the plotter.","section":"tasks","order":5,"keywords":["operating point","transient","AC sweep","meter","scope","plotter","bode","frequency response"],"headings":[{"id":"the-three-analyses","text":"The three analyses","level":2},{"id":"current","text":"Current","level":2},{"id":"what-is-checked","text":"What is checked","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"the-three-analyses\"\u003EThe three analyses\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOperating point.\u003C/strong\u003E The DC solution: every source at its DC value, capacitors open, inductors shorted. The result is a table of net voltages and part currents. A \u003Cstrong\u003Emeter\u003C/strong\u003E shows the voltage of its net on the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETransient.\u003C/strong\u003E The circuit over time, from the operating point. Type the stop time and the step. A \u003Cstrong\u003Escope\u003C/strong\u003E shows the voltage of the net it sits on; put several scopes to see several nets. The trace joins the solved points; nothing is smoothed. A smaller step is closer to the exact answer, and a very small step on a long time is refused (over 20000 steps).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAC sweep.\u003C/strong\u003E The small-signal response at the operating point, from a start to an end frequency with points per decade. A source needs an AC size (an AC source has one; a DC or pulse source has an &quot;AC size for the sweep&quot; value). A \u003Cstrong\u003Eplotter\u003C/strong\u003E shows the level (dB relative to 1 V) and the phase of its net. With a 1 V AC source the level is the gain.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"current\"\u003ECurrent\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe current of a part is shown in the operating point table, from its first pin to its second. For a source it runs from the + pin to the − pin through the source, so a source that delivers power reads negative.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-checked\"\u003EWhat is checked\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe analyses do not run while the rule check has an error. See the checks topic.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The three analyses Operating point. The DC solution: every source at its DC value, capacitors open, inductors shorted. The result is a table of net voltages and part currents. A meter shows the voltage of its net on the drawing. Transient. The circuit over time, from the operating point. Type the stop time and the step. A scope shows the voltage of the net it sits on; put several scopes to see several nets. The trace joins the solved points; nothing is smoothed. A smaller step is closer to the exact answer, and a very small step on a long time is refused (over 20000 steps). AC sweep. The small-signal response at the operating point, from a start to an end frequency with points per decade. A source needs an AC size (an AC source has one; a DC or pulse source has an \"AC size for the sweep\" value). A plotter shows the level (dB relative to 1 V) and the phase of its net. With a 1 V AC source the level is the gain. Current The current of a part is shown in the operating point table, from its first pin to its second. For a source it runs from the + pin to the − pin through the source, so a source that delivers power reads negative. What is checked The analyses do not run while the rule check has an error. See the checks topic."},{"slug":"circuit-parts","scope":"addon:circuit","title":"Circuit simulation: parts and values","summary":"The parts in the Circuit simulation palette, what each value means, and how to type a value.","section":"reference","order":10,"keywords":["parts","values","resistor","capacitor","inductor","diode","LED","transistor","MOSFET","op-amp","switch","source","units"],"headings":[{"id":"typing-a-value","text":"Typing a value","level":2},{"id":"the-parts","text":"The parts","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"typing-a-value\"\u003ETyping a value\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou type every value. Use a number with an optional suffix: \u003Cstrong\u003Ek\u003C/strong\u003E thousand, \u003Cstrong\u003Emeg\u003C/strong\u003E million, \u003Cstrong\u003Em\u003C/strong\u003E milli, \u003Cstrong\u003Eu\u003C/strong\u003E micro, \u003Cstrong\u003En\u003C/strong\u003E nano, \u003Cstrong\u003Ep\u003C/strong\u003E pico, \u003Cstrong\u003Ef\u003C/strong\u003E femto, \u003Cstrong\u003Eg\u003C/strong\u003E giga, \u003Cstrong\u003Et\u003C/strong\u003E tera. Write 4.7k, 100n, 1meg, 2.2u, 1e-14. A capital M also means milli, as in SPICE; write meg for a million. Letters after the suffix (a unit such as F or ohm) are ignored. A value that is not a number is marked red and is a problem in Problems.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-parts\"\u003EThe parts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EResistor, capacitor, inductor.\u003C/strong\u003E The value is in ohms, farads, henries. A capacitor and an inductor can have a start value (volts, amps) that is used if you type one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESwitch.\u003C/strong\u003E Closed it is a resistor with the on resistance (1 mΩ by default); open, the off resistance (1 GΩ). It is not a model of a real contact.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDC source.\u003C/strong\u003E Volts. Its AC size, if not zero, is the amplitude it adds in the AC sweep.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAC source.\u003C/strong\u003E A sine with offset, amplitude and frequency. In the AC sweep it is a source of that amplitude.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPulse source.\u003C/strong\u003E Low and high level, delay, rise and fall time, width and period.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECurrent source.\u003C/strong\u003E Amps, flowing through the source from the + pin to the − pin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDiode and LED.\u003C/strong\u003E The diode equation with a saturation current and an emission factor. The defaults are textbook figures (1e-14 A and 1 for a diode; 1e-18 A and 2 for an LED, which drops about 1.9 V at 10 mA). They are not any particular part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENPN and PNP transistor.\u003C/strong\u003E Ebers–Moll transport model with a saturation current and forward and reverse gain.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EN and P MOSFET.\u003C/strong\u003E The first-order square-law model: transconductance (the width-to-length ratio is already in it), threshold voltage and a channel-length factor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOp-amp.\u003C/strong\u003E A voltage amplifier with a finite open-loop gain (100000 by default), no input current and no supply rails: it does not saturate. Use the gain to see what a real amplifier&#039;s finite gain does to your circuit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGround, junction.\u003C/strong\u003E Wiring only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMeter, scope, plotter.\u003C/strong\u003E Instruments; see Analyses.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENone of the values comes from a maker&#039;s data sheet or model. If you want a particular part, type its figures.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Typing a value You type every value. Use a number with an optional suffix: k thousand, meg million, m milli, u micro, n nano, p pico, f femto, g giga, t tera. Write 4.7k, 100n, 1meg, 2.2u, 1e-14. A capital M also means milli, as in SPICE; write meg for a million. Letters after the suffix (a unit such as F or ohm) are ignored. A value that is not a number is marked red and is a problem in Problems. The parts Resistor, capacitor, inductor. The value is in ohms, farads, henries. A capacitor and an inductor can have a start value (volts, amps) that is used if you type one. Switch. Closed it is a resistor with the on resistance (1 mΩ by default); open, the off resistance (1 GΩ). It is not a model of a real contact. DC source. Volts. Its AC size, if not zero, is the amplitude it adds in the AC sweep. AC source. A sine with offset, amplitude and frequency. In the AC sweep it is a source of that amplitude. Pulse source. Low and high level, delay, rise and fall time, width and period. Current source. Amps, flowing through the source from the + pin to the − pin. Diode and LED. The diode equation with a saturation current and an emission factor. The defaults are textbook figures (1e-14 A and 1 for a diode; 1e-18 A and 2 for an LED, which drops about 1.9 V at 10 mA). They are not any particular part. NPN and PNP transistor. Ebers–Moll transport model with a saturation current and forward and reverse gain. N and P MOSFET. The first-order square-law model: transconductance (the width-to-length ratio is already in it), threshold voltage and a channel-length factor. Op-amp. A voltage amplifier with a finite open-loop gain (100000 by default), no input current and no supply rails: it does not saturate. Use the gain to see what a real amplifier's finite gain does to your circuit. Ground, junction. Wiring only. Meter, scope, plotter. Instruments; see Analyses. None of the values comes from a maker's data sheet or model. If you want a particular part, type its figures."},{"slug":"circuit-checks","scope":"addon:circuit","title":"Circuit simulation: the checks","summary":"What the Circuit simulation add-on warns about in Problems, and how to fix each.","section":"reference","order":20,"keywords":["problems","checks","ground","floating","loop","short","value"],"headings":[{"id":"what-is-checked","text":"What is checked","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-checked\"\u003EWhat is checked\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe checks show in the Problems panel and update when you change the drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo ground.\u003C/strong\u003E An error. Place a Ground part, or name a net 0 or gnd.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA pin that is not connected.\u003C/strong\u003E A warning. Wire it, or remove the part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETwo parts with one name.\u003C/strong\u003E An error. Rename one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETwo net names joined by wiring.\u003C/strong\u003E An error: one of the nets is a short, or a name is wrong.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA value that is not a number\u003C/strong\u003E, or a resistance, capacitance or inductance at or below zero. An error that quotes the value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA net with no DC path to ground.\u003C/strong\u003E A warning, for example the node between two capacitors in series. Its voltage is not fixed by the circuit; add a resistor to ground. The analyses still run.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA loop of voltage sources and inductors\u003C/strong\u003E, or a source with both ends on one net. An error: the current in the loop is undefined. Put a small resistor in it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach problem names the part, so selecting it in Problems finds it.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What is checked The checks show in the Problems panel and update when you change the drawing. No ground. An error. Place a Ground part, or name a net 0 or gnd. A pin that is not connected. A warning. Wire it, or remove the part. Two parts with one name. An error. Rename one. Two net names joined by wiring. An error: one of the nets is a short, or a name is wrong. A value that is not a number, or a resistance, capacitance or inductance at or below zero. An error that quotes the value. A net with no DC path to ground. A warning, for example the node between two capacitors in series. Its voltage is not fixed by the circuit; add a resistor to ground. The analyses still run. A loop of voltage sources and inductors, or a source with both ends on one net. An error: the current in the loop is undefined. Put a small resistor in it. Each problem names the part, so selecting it in Problems finds it."},{"slug":"circuit-limits","scope":"addon:circuit","title":"Circuit simulation: what it does not do","summary":"What the Circuit simulation add-on does not do and how far to trust it, including that Multisim files and part models are not read.","section":"trouble","order":90,"keywords":["limits","not built","multisim","spice","models","accuracy"],"headings":[{"id":"what-is-not-there","text":"What is not there","level":2},{"id":"how-far-to-trust-it","text":"How far to trust it","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-not-there\"\u003EWhat is not there\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOther programs&#039; files.\u003C/strong\u003E Multisim files and part models are not read, and nothing here is promised about them. The add-on has its own file (Save and Open) and writes a plain text netlist of the elements.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EReal part models.\u003C/strong\u003E There is no library of real transistors, op-amps or diodes. You type the figures of the part you mean. The op-amp has no supply rails, so it never saturates, and no bandwidth limit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMore analyses.\u003C/strong\u003E No noise, temperature sweep, Monte Carlo, Fourier or DC sweep in the panel yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOther instruments.\u003C/strong\u003E The meter reads DC voltage; there is no wattmeter, signal generator window or logic analyser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBoards.\u003C/strong\u003E Printed circuit boards are not part of this add-on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-far-to-trust-it\"\u003EHow far to trust it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe solver is our own, written from the published method (modified nodal analysis with Newton iteration). It has been checked against closed forms: a divider, an RC step, a series RLC resonance, a half-wave rectifier, an op-amp gain stage, a MOSFET, a transistor bias and an LED. Each is a small case. A circuit with sharp switching or a very wide range of time constants may fail to converge; then it says so and does not give a guess.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is for learning, checking an idea and designing small circuits. It is not a sign-off tool, and the answers are only as good as the values you typed.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What is not there Other programs' files. Multisim files and part models are not read, and nothing here is promised about them. The add-on has its own file (Save and Open) and writes a plain text netlist of the elements. Real part models. There is no library of real transistors, op-amps or diodes. You type the figures of the part you mean. The op-amp has no supply rails, so it never saturates, and no bandwidth limit. More analyses. No noise, temperature sweep, Monte Carlo, Fourier or DC sweep in the panel yet. Other instruments. The meter reads DC voltage; there is no wattmeter, signal generator window or logic analyser. Boards. Printed circuit boards are not part of this add-on. How far to trust it The solver is our own, written from the published method (modified nodal analysis with Newton iteration). It has been checked against closed forms: a divider, an RC step, a series RLC resonance, a half-wave rectifier, an op-amp gain stage, a MOSFET, a transistor bias and an LED. Each is a small case. A circuit with sharp switching or a very wide range of time constants may fail to converge; then it says so and does not give a guess. It is for learning, checking an idea and designing small circuits. It is not a sign-off tool, and the answers are only as good as the values you typed."}]};
